userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add terminal advisory retirement and immutable disposition stamps to recurrence classification.

**Motivation** — carried sub-threshold findings have no exit.

**Approach** — The recurrence count is computed for every finding; the severity gate then selects which cap applies.

`meta.recurrence` is written by `classifyRecurrence` on every finding it returns:

```
{
  disposition: "blocking" | "advisory" | "demoted" | "retired",
  rounds: number,          // this finding's appearance count including this round
  wasBlocking: boolean     // severity was at or above the blocking threshold
}
```

**Scope** — In: recurrence result/candidate/config types, recurrence algorithm, review schemas and inline config shapes. Out: mapper and review-operation propagation (US-002); prompt rendering and fix-lane seeding (US-003); Similarity-clustering or otherwise changing `fingerprintFor`. Retirement inherits whatever recurrence identity that function already provides.

**Interface** —
```typescript
export type RecurrenceConfig = { enabled: boolean; maxBlockingRounds: number; maxAdvisoryRounds?: number };
export type RecurrenceResult<T = AdversarialLLMFinding> = {
  blocking: T[];
  advisory: T[];
  demoted: T[];
  retired: T[];
  classified: T[];
};
export interface RecurrenceCandidate {
  severity: string;
  file: string;
  issue: string;
  category?: string;
  acIndex?: number;
  meta?: Record<string, unknown>;
}
```

The field is **optional in the type**, defaulted to `2` inside `classifyRecurrence`. Making it required would break 25 construction sites across `src/` and `test/`. The config schema still declares it with its own default, so configuration remains the source of the value.
